Implement user story AMSD-2041-A: Live Preview Infrastructure — Config, Connection, and Hook Integration

## Story Description
Wire the Contentstack Live Preview SDK into the existing Stack initialization and useContent hook so that live preview events trigger content re-fetches. This covers the shared infrastructure that all three brands (GO, UP, MX) depend on: environment toggle, connection lifecycle, and content update propagation through useContent.

## Acceptance Criteria
- When a content author edits content in Contentstack CMS, the live preview renders the updated content in the application without requiring a full page refresh
- Live Preview can be enabled or disabled via the CONTENTSTACK_LIVE_PREVIEW environment variable
- When the Live Preview connection to Contentstack is lost, the page continues to display the last known content without throwing errors
- Live Preview does not degrade application performance when the feature is disabled in production


## Root Cause Analysis & Prescribed Fix (AUTHORITATIVE — start here, do not re-trace)
A code investigation already traced this bug to its cause and prescribed the minimal fix below. This is the plan of record. Apply it; do NOT re-read the whole codebase to re-derive it.

The Acceptance Criteria above describe the desired END BEHAVIOR to VERIFY — they are NOT an implementation blueprint. Do not re-architect, split values, or add new fields/abstractions to satisfy an AC literally when the prescribed minimal fix already makes that AC pass. Implement the fix below; the ACs are how you check you got it right.

HARD RULES:
- Make the SMALLEST change that fixes the root cause. Fewer lines of code is always better.
- REUSE existing functions. Before writing any new helper, search the repo for an existing util/parser/formatter that already does what you need (use the CodeGraph tool documented below) and call it. Writing novel code when a helper already exists is a defect to be rejected in review.
- **src/context/ContentstackContext.tsx** (`ContentstackProvider`): This is where content becomes static — it receives defaultContent from getStaticProps and memoizes it once. For Live Preview to work, the provider must subscribe to Contentstack's onEntryChange events and reactively update the context when the CMS sends draft content changes. Currently the useMemo on defaultContent (line 56-61) creates a frozen context that never responds to live preview updates, which is the root cause of live preview being non-functional.
  - **Minimal fix:** Replace the static useMemo with useState initialized from defaultContent, then subscribe to Contentstack live preview events (Stack.livePreviewInit / onEntryChange) inside a useEffect to call setContent with updated draft content. The Stack object and its livePreviewInit method are already available from src/services/contentstack.ts (exported as Stack). Import Stack from '../../services/contentstack' and call Stack.livePreviewInit({ enable: true }) in the effect, then use its onEntryChange callback to re-fetch or merge updated content into state. The live_preview config is already set in the Stack options (src/services/contentstack.ts lines 69-72), so only the client-side subscription wiring in ContentstackProvider is missing.


## Verification Criteria (what a tester will CONFIRM — your change must satisfy every one)
These are observable checks, derived from the acceptance criteria and description. They describe WHAT is observed, not how to build it. Make the minimal change that makes all of these true; your accompanying test should assert them:
- Given a page is open in the browser with live preview enabled, when a tester edits a content entry in the Contentstack CMS and saves the change, then the updated content appears on the open page without the tester manually refreshing the browser.
- Given live preview is enabled for a page, when a tester views that page, then the page displays the current draft (unpublished) version of the content as it exists in the CMS, rather than the last published version.
- Given live preview is disabled for a page, when a tester views that page, then the page displays only the published version of the content and no unpublished draft changes are visible.
- Given a page is open with live preview enabled, when a tester changes a text field of the displayed entry in the CMS, then the new text is visible on the open page within a short time and the old text is no longer shown.
- Given a page is open with live preview enabled, when a tester makes several consecutive edits to the entry in the CMS, then each successive version of the content appears on the open page in turn, ending with the most recent edit displayed.
- Given a page is open with live preview enabled, when a tester reverts an edit in the CMS back to its previous value, then the page updates to show the reverted (previous) content without a manual browser refresh.
- Given a page is open with live preview enabled, when the content source becomes unavailable, then the page continues to display the last successfully loaded content and does not show a blank screen, broken layout, or an unhandled error message to the user.
- Given a page is open with live preview enabled during an editing session, when content updates arrive from the CMS, then the page updates in place without a full page reload, visible flicker, or repeated reloading loop.

